<BaseLayout title="About" description="Why Family Cabinet records the things a family makes, keeps and collects.">
  <header class="page-heading wrap"><p class="eyebrow">The idea behind the cabinet</p><h1>About the archive</h1><p class="page-intro">A small, growing record of objects and the lives around them.</p></header>
  <section class="about-body wrap"><h2>What we keep</h2><div class="about-copy"><p>Family Cabinet is a living digital archive of things a family makes, keeps and collects. Instead of treating objects as products, it records what they are, where they came from, why they matter and how their place in the family changes over time.</p><p>The project evolved from an earlier ecommerce demo into an experiment in personal information architecture. It asks how a useful catalogue might hold both practical details and the quieter context around an object.</p><p class="about-small">This public version contains fictional demonstration records. A future family space could keep private material separate, with deliberate access controls.</p></div></section>
</BaseLayout>
